-- A rule that lacks a name the site writes still counts as a reading of the
-- site, so a default is not taken to choose the other rule. Only a value that
-- does not fit the type of an input the rule HAS rules it out
-- (ok/typically-overload-fit.l4). Each site below is ambiguous, as it was
-- before TYPICALLY.

-- 1. The author means the flat fee, the second rule, and writes `amount`, the
-- first rule's spelling of its input, by mistake. If the second rule were ruled
-- out for not having `amount`, the first would be run on a presumed `rate`, and
-- the misspelling would go unreported.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
      currency IS A STRING
      rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 0.02
GIVETH A NUMBER
fee MEANS amount TIMES rate

GIVEN amt IS A NUMBER
      currency IS A STRING
GIVETH A NUMBER
fee MEANS 5

#EVAL fee WITH amount IS 100, currency IS "SGD"

-- 2. The other rule shares no name with the site at all. It is still a reading
-- the author may have meant, and the site does not say which.
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 3
      base IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled MEANS rate TIMES base

GIVEN width IS A NUMBER
      height IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled MEANS width TIMES height

#EVAL scaled WITH base IS 10
